<InsightArticle title={e.title} description={e.description} date="2026-11-14" type={e.type as 'Analysis' | 'Explainer'} readTime="5 min" standfirst="At the assumed rates and token caps here, twenty model calls reserve $0.18. That bound holds only if the application counts calls and enforces the caps, including recovery attempts.">

<p>“Keep costs low” is an instruction, not an accounting system. For an agent that can continue calling a model and tools, the application needs a rule that decides whether another operation may start. Put that rule outside the model's judgement about its own progress.</p>
<p>The <a href={e.sources[0].url}>Claude tool-use overview</a>, read on 14 November 2026, describes the boundary for client tools: the model proposes a structured call, your application executes it and returns the result. That is a useful place to enforce operation limits. It is also where a tool proposal can be recorded without automatically carrying it out.</p>
<h2>Build the reserve from maxima</h2>
<p>Assume a maximum of 4,000 input tokens and 1,000 billable output tokens per model call. At invented rates of $1 per million input tokens and $5 per million output tokens, one call costs at most $0.009 under those assumptions. All billable output must fit the stated limit.</p>
<WorkedTable headers={["Allowed model calls","Maximum input per call","Maximum billable output per call","Nominal reserve, USD"]} rows={examples['agent-budget-enforced-stop']} caption="Synthetic fixed-rate bound. Model calls only, at assumed $1 input and $5 output per million tokens; external operations are excluded." />
<p>These are deliberately maxima, not average requests multiplied by a step count. An average can help forecast a likely bill. It cannot bound an unusually long run. The table also assumes a flat rate with no extra fees, no uncounted retries and no overlapping calls.</p>
<h2>Reserve before sending</h2>
<p>Before starting a request, check the remaining budget and reserve enough for that request's allowed maximum. Reconcile the reservation against reported usage afterwards. If several calls can run in parallel, make their reservations together so they cannot each spend the same remaining amount.</p>
<p>Give recovery attempts entries in the same ledger. A request repeated inside an SDK still belongs to the job even if your outer loop did not advance. Verify the actual attempt behaviour in a small controlled run before relying on a loop counter as a call counter.</p>
<p>Keep a separate allowance for external operations. A search, storage write or paid tool may have its own billing unit. Do not present a model-token bound as a total spending cap when those operations can continue outside it.</p>
<h2>Choose a useful stop outcome</h2>
<p>Define what the agent returns when it reaches a limit: the work completed, what remains, the reason it stopped and any evidence needed to resume. A stopped job is not automatically a failed implementation. It may be the correct outcome when the task is larger than the authorised budget.</p>
<p>Allow room for that final report if it needs another model call. Alternatively, make the application produce the stop message from the ledger. A design that spends the entire reserve on exploration and then requires an unbudgeted explanation has not finished its accounting.</p>
<p>Use a deadline alongside token and call caps. Waiting for slow tools can exhaust the useful time without exhausting tokens. Record which limit stopped the run so you can distinguish a budget problem from a scheduling problem.</p>
<h2>Inspect progress separately from activity</h2>
<p>A tool call is activity. Define a progress event in terms of the task: a verified file change, an accepted field or a completed check. If the loop repeats without a new progress event, let the application pause or request a narrower scope. Keep that policy explicit rather than asking the model to judge endlessly whether it is almost done.</p>
<p>Test the stop path with synthetic tools and deliberately small caps. Confirm that the next operation is denied and that the report retains completed work. Do this without involving real payments or consequential writes.</p>
<p>The tracker entry should name the enforced limits, the ledger's scope and the stop evidence. Then a reader can tell whether a budget was an estimate, a prompt request or an actual boundary.</p>
